          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Application Differences</a>
          <a href="#chemistry" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Chemistry Families</a>
          <a href="#brick-and-stone" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Brick and Natural Stone</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Both are masonry sealers and both draw on the same families of chemistry. The difference is what they are asked to do: a concrete sealer protects one continuous slab, while a paver sealer has to protect many small units and the sand-filled joints between them.
        </p>

        <div id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Paver Sealer vs Concrete Sealer at a Glance
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Paver Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete Sealer</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many units plus sand joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One continuous slab</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Handles joint sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes; some formulations bind it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour enhancement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common and often the point</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Available but less emphasised</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Efflorescence tolerance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Formulated for it; pavers bloom readily</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less emphasised</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical chemistry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic, urethane, some penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silane, siloxane, acrylic, epoxy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 5 years, film-forming</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 to 10 years depending on type</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Slip resistance additives</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly offered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less commonly needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reapplication</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recoat over sound film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recoat or re-treat depending on type</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Practical Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">On pavers, use a paver sealer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The chemistry families overlap enough that a generic concrete sealer will physically work on pavers, but paver-specific products are formulated for the two things pavers do that slabs do not — throw efflorescence, and have joints. Those are the failure modes you will actually meet, so use the product built for them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Joints Are the Real Difference
          </h2>

          <p>
            A concrete slab is one continuous surface with control joints cut into it. A paver installation is hundreds of separate units with sand between them, and that sand is both structurally important and vulnerable. Sealer formulation has to account for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Stabilising Formulations</h3>

          <p>
            Some paver sealers are designed to penetrate joint sand and bind it in place as well as coating the paver surface. There is no equivalent product in the concrete sealer category because there is nothing to bind. If your joints are loose or lightly eroded, this is a category that exists specifically for your problem — and no concrete sealer will address it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture Escape Through Joints</h3>

          <p>
            Joints are also a moisture path. A paver installation breathes through its joints in a way a sealed slab does not, and paver sealers are generally formulated with that in mind. A very impermeable coating that works fine on a slab can trap moisture rising through paver joints, producing the cloudy blush that is the classic paver sealing failure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Application Around Joints</h3>

          <p>
            There is a practical difference too. Sealing pavers means the product runs into the joints whether you intend it to or not, so the sand needs to be in place, cured and dry before you start. On a slab there is no such interaction — you are coating a surface and nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="efflorescence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Efflorescence: A Paver Problem More Than a Slab Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Efflorescence is the white, powdery mineral bloom that appears on masonry as water moves through it and evaporates at the surface, leaving dissolved salts behind. Concrete pavers are notably prone to it, particularly when new, and it is one of the main reasons paver sealers are formulated differently.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>New pavers commonly bloom during their first year as they cure and release free salts.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sealing over active efflorescence traps it under the film, where it is essentially unreachable.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A sealer that is too impermeable can worsen it by concentrating moisture movement.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Many paver sealers are formulated to remain breathable enough to let this moisture pass.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Efflorescence cleaners are a normal part of paver preparation and rarely feature in slab work.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The practical implication is that preparation before sealing pavers involves a step that concrete slab work generally does not: treating efflorescence, and — with a new installation — waiting long enough that the initial bloom has finished before sealing at all. Sealing new pavers too early is a common way to lock a white haze under the film permanently.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A Note on New Pavers</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Let a new installation weather before sealing.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">New concrete pavers frequently throw efflorescence during their first months. Sealing immediately traps it. Allowing an installation to weather through a season, cleaning off any bloom that appears, then sealing gives a far better and more predictable result than sealing the week it was laid.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Product for Which Surface
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete pavers, patio</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paver sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joints, efflorescence, colour enhancement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clay brick pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paver or brick sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Similar needs; check the product names brick</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Natural stone pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stone-specific sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some stone is acid-sensitive and stains differently</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Poured concrete driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating concrete sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No joints; freeze-thaw and salt are the enemy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stamped or decorative concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film-forming concrete sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Appearance is part of the point</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete garage floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or urethane coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Different demands; chemical and abrasion resistance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mixed paver and concrete area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Correct product on each</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Or an acrylic explicitly rated for both</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Overlap Is Genuine</h3>

          <p>
            Acrylic sealers are the honest overlap. Many acrylics are rated for both concrete and pavers and will state so on the label, and on a mixed area — a paver patio meeting a concrete apron, say — such a product is the sensible choice for a consistent finish. The rule is to read the label rather than to assume: a product that does not name your surface is not for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where It Is Not</h3>

          <p>
            Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers are the concrete specialists, and on an exposed slab facing winter salt they are the durable answer at five to ten years. On pavers they are a legitimate choice for stain resistance without appearance change, but they will not enhance colour and they do nothing for joint sand — so if either of those is why you were sealing, they are the wrong category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Application Differs in Practice
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pavers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete Slab</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface prep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, treat efflorescence, check joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, etch or profile if required</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand must be installed and cured first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Waiting period</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 days after polymeric sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After the concrete has cured, typically 28 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drying before sealing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full dry day; joints hold moisture longest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full dry day; slab holds moisture in its mass</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Application method</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprayer or roller; product runs into joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprayer or roller across a continuous surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recoating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Over a sound film after cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Depends on type; penetrating may just be re-treated</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The two rows that have no slab equivalent are the ones that catch people out. Joint sand has to be installed and fully cured before any sealer goes down, and the 30-day polymeric sand cure is a genuine constraint on project scheduling that does not exist when you are sealing a slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shared lesson is about moisture. Both surfaces hold water far longer than their appearance suggests — a slab in its mass, a paver installation in its joints — and both produce the same cloudy failure when a sealer film goes over water that has not finished leaving. Allowing a genuine dry period before sealing is the single most transferable piece of advice between the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Chemistry Families, and Which Cross Over
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both categories draw on the same handful of chemistries. Knowing which is which makes the labels far easier to read.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chemistry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Used On</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Behaviour</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Life</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic, water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film-forming, easy recoat, mild colour lift</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic, solvent-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film-forming, stronger colour enhancement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Urethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tough film, good abrasion resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane / siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating, no film, no colour change</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete, interior</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very hard film, not UV stable outdoors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years indoors</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylics Are the Crossover Category</h3>

          <p>
            This is why so many products are labelled for both surfaces: acrylic sealers work on concrete and pavers alike, and the differences between a paver-branded acrylic and a concrete-branded one are often about additives — joint stabilising resins, efflorescence tolerance, slip resistance — rather than the base chemistry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Epoxy Belongs Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            Worth flagging because it is widely sold and frequently misapplied. Most epoxy formulations are not UV stable and will amber and chalk in direct sunlight, which makes them excellent for a garage floor and a poor choice for a patio or driveway of either material.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brick-and-stone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where Brick and Natural Stone Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of this comparison assumes concrete pavers, which are the common case. Clay brick and natural stone behave differently enough to be worth separating out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clay Brick Pavers</h3>

          <p>
            Clay brick is more porous than concrete and takes sealer readily, sometimes too readily — an unsealed clay paver can absorb a great deal of product on the first coat. It also throws less efflorescence than concrete, because the salts that cause it largely come from cement. Products labelled for brick account for the absorption, and a brick-specific or paver sealer rated for clay is the right choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural Stone</h3>

          <p>
            Natural stone is the category where generic products most often go wrong, because stone varies enormously. Limestone and travertine are acid-sensitive and can be etched by cleaners that concrete shrugs off. Slate flakes if moisture is trapped beneath a film. Dense granite barely absorbs anything and needs a product formulated for low-porosity stone.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Use a sealer that names your specific stone type where possible, not a generic masonry product.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Test in an inconspicuous area and wait a full day — some sealers darken stone dramatically.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Avoid acidic cleaners on limestone, travertine and marble entirely.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Prefer breathable or penetrating products on flaky stone such as slate.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Expect to reseal natural stone on its own schedule rather than the concrete paver interval.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The general principle across all three materials is the same: the more unusual your surface, the more the label matters and the more a test patch is worth doing. A product that does not name your material is not formulated for it, however similar the surfaces look.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Generic Concrete Sealer on Pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It will physically work, but it is not formulated for the two things pavers actually do — bloom efflorescence and have joints. You lose access to joint stabilising formulations, and a very impermeable product can trap moisture rising through the joints and produce the classic cloudy blush.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Pavers Immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New concrete pavers commonly throw efflorescence through their first months as they release free salts. Sealing straight away locks that white bloom under the film where it is effectively unreachable. Let the installation weather, clean off any bloom, and seal once it has settled.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Penetrating Sealer to Enhance Colour</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers line the pore structure rather than forming a surface film, so they protect well and change appearance very little. If you were sealing for the wet-look colour enhancement, this category will disappoint you regardless of how durable it is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Pavers Before the Joint Sand Has Cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer runs into the joints whether you intend it or not, so the sand must be installed and fully cured first — about 30 days for polymeric sand. Sealing over sand still releasing moisture traps it under the film and clouds the finish across the whole installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using One Product Across a Mixed Paver and Concrete Area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A paver patio meeting a concrete apron needs the correct product on each section, unless you have specifically found an acrylic labelled for both surfaces. Using one product across both for a uniform look means half the area is protected by the wrong formulation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between paver sealer and concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are masonry sealers drawing on overlapping chemistry, but paver sealers are formulated for two things slabs do not have: sand-filled joints, and a strong tendency to throw efflorescence. Some paver sealers also bind joint sand, which has no concrete equivalent because there is nothing to bind.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use concrete sealer on pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will physically work, but it is not the right tool. You lose joint stabilising formulations, and products formulated for slabs can be less breathable than a paver installation needs, trapping moisture that rises through the joints and producing a cloudy finish. Use a product that names pavers on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use paver sealer on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes — many acrylic paver sealers are rated for concrete as well and will say so on the label. That makes them a sensible choice on a mixed area where a paver patio meets a concrete apron. Check that the label names concrete rather than assuming.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which lasts longer, paver sealer or concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating concrete sealers generally last longest at five to ten years, because the protection sits inside the pore structure where traffic cannot wear it. Film-forming paver sealers typically run two to five years. The comparison is really between penetrating and film-forming rather than between pavers and concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a joint stabilising sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A paver sealer formulated to penetrate joint sand and bind it in place as well as coating the paver surface. It has no concrete sealer equivalent because a slab has no joint sand. It is the practical answer for a sealed patio whose joints have eroded but are not empty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a different sealer for natural stone pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. Natural stone varies considerably and some types are acid-sensitive or stain differently from concrete pavers, so a stone-specific sealer is the safer choice. Check that the product names your stone type, and test in an inconspicuous area before doing the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is paver sealer more expensive than concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Broadly comparable within the same chemistry — a solvent-based acrylic costs similar amounts whichever surface it is branded for. The larger price differences come from choosing between chemistries, with penetrating silane and siloxane products typically costing more upfront but lasting considerably longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a driveway sealer on my paver patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if it is an asphalt driveway sealer — that is a black coating formulated to bond with bitumen and it has no place on pavers. A concrete driveway sealer is closer, and if it is an acrylic rated for pavers it may be fine, but check that the label names pavers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pavers need sealing at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, it is optional. Sealing adds stain resistance and colour enhancement and can help stabilise joint sand, but pavers do not need it structurally and perform well unsealed. It is also a recurring commitment — choosing to seal means choosing a maintenance cycle every few years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

