        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            These are closer relatives than asphalt and concrete sealers, drawing on overlapping chemistry, but they are built for different jobs. A concrete sealer protects one continuous slab. A paver sealer protects many small units and has to cope with the sand-filled joints between them and the efflorescence that concrete pavers readily throw.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On pavers, use a paver sealer. The joint stabilising formulations that bind loose joint sand have no concrete equivalent, and products built for slabs can be less breathable than a paver installation needs — which is how moisture rising through joints ends up trapped under a film as cloudiness.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The genuine overlap is acrylics, many of which are rated for both and will say so, making them the right answer where a paver patio meets a concrete apron. And the one lesson that transfers completely: both surfaces hold water far longer than they look like they do, and sealing before that water has left is the most common way to ruin either one.
          </p>
        </div>

